WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:09.420
JCL statements consist of one or more 80 byte records, however, be careful when you get

2
00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:16.260
close to the end of that line there, because columns 73 through 80 are ignored by zos and

3
00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:19.180
are usually used for sequence numbers.

4
00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:22.740
If you've got a command with a whole lot of parameters that you need to continue onto

5
00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:25.860
the second line, here's what you do.

6
00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:29.140
And note, this is only how you continue for a parameter.

7
00:00:29.140 --> 00:00:34.580
If you've got a comment or a parameter field enclosed in parentheses, those require something

8
00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:36.380
slightly different.

9
00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:42.540
But if you've got a list of parameters, interrupt the field after a complete parameter entry,

10
00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:44.780
making sure to end it with a comma.

11
00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:49.060
That comma needs to come at or before column 71.

12
00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:53.780
In the next line, which you're going to be continuing into, start it with two forward

13
00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:56.560
slashes and then a blank space.

14
00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:03.060
If column three starts with anything other than a blank space or an asterisk, splat,

15
00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:06.840
it will assume it's a new line and probably won't run right.

16
00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:11.120
You can then pick up your continued command starting anywhere from columns four through

17
00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:12.320
16.

18
00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:17.800
You should also know that there's different types of statements, job, exec, and dd.

19
00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:21.680
And you can see all three types here, and they all have parameters that are expected

20
00:01:21.680 --> 00:01:23.280
to follow them.

21
00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:27.640
I also want to talk to you about those dispositions you see here, because you'll definitely be

22
00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:29.240
running into those.

23
00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:32.840
But before we can get into that, we have to cover cataloging.

24
00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:38.880
Now cataloging a dataset just means that we add the exact location of that dataset so

25
00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:43.440
we can find it later, referencing it only by its name.

26
00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:47.240
Datasets can exist without being in a catalog, you just have to know exactly where they are

27
00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:48.800
to use them.

28
00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:53.360
Having a dataset in your catalog is like having a phone number in your address book.

29
00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:58.840
So if you want to reference a dataset by its name later on, make sure it's in the catalog.

30
00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:00.180
That's it.

31
00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:05.800
When you specify the disposition in a dd statement, you're specifying what to do with the datasets

32
00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:07.440
in JCL.

33
00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:11.080
And you do this across three fields separated by a comma.

34
00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:14.000
And don't worry, there is an example after this.

35
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:17.600
You start with DISP equals, that sets it all up.

36
00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:22.120
In the first field, we're specifying whether it should be new, either it doesn't exist

37
00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:28.760
yet so we should make it, old, meaning it should already exist, share, the data can

38
00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:34.840
be used by other jobs, and mod, if the dataset doesn't already exist, then create it.

39
00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:38.540
And if it already does exist, then use that.

40
00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:43.760
In the next statement, we specify what action the system needs to perform on the dataset

41
00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:46.800
when the job step terminates normally.

42
00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:52.960
And we can either delete it, get rid of it, keep it, and if you're referencing a dataset

43
00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:58.520
that you expected to be there when it started, you probably want to keep it around, pass,

44
00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:02.640
meaning that if there's a step after this step that needs the dataset, let them use

45
00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:09.000
it, catalog, add it to the catalog, make it permanent, or uncatalog, remove it from the

46
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:10.520
catalog.

47
00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:15.480
And then lastly, in the third column, what to do if a job step terminates abnormally,

48
00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:17.720
if something bad happens.

49
00:03:17.720 --> 00:03:22.960
Same as the previous column, but there's no option for pass because if the job is stopping,

50
00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:25.800
there are no future steps to pass it on to.

51
00:03:25.800 --> 00:03:32.040
So here's an example of a DD statement with a disposition of new catalog delete.

52
00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:36.360
We're going to create a new dataset, and if the job runs successfully, add it to the

53
00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:39.320
catalog, otherwise delete it.

54
00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:44.380
I know this was a lot of information, and believe me, I wish that were everything.

55
00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:49.820
For the full details on JES and JCL, refer to the user guide and reference linked in

56
00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:50.580
this lesson.
